    "A": "Research a serial O(n²) column-first streaming solver. Maintain n vertical sums for the latest s rows, retaining raw rows in an s-row circular buffer and subtracting expired entries before overwriting them. For each complete vertical window, scan length-s horizontal windows over those column sums. Use contiguous storage, bounded allocations and wide arithmetic. Compare fused parsing/updates with simple contiguous update loops. Deliver a complete correct candidate and measurements isolating algorithmic gains.",
    "B": "Research CPU parallelism using at most four workers supported by the unchanged build. Read a flat row-major matrix and partition candidate top-left rows into independent bands. Each worker initializes private column sums at its band's start, slides vertically, evaluates horizontal windows and produces a private maximum; reduce once afterward. Account for duplicated initialization work and thread-launch overhead. Compare serial, two-worker and four-worker variants within the existing budget, retaining a general dimension/work-based serial fallback where justified. Avoid per-row synchronization and shared hot-loop maxima.",
    "C": "Research an input- and memory-centric horizontal-first streaming alternative. Let m=n-s+1. Form each row's m length-s sums, retain s rows of these sums in an int64 circular buffer, and maintain m vertical totals. This provides O(n²) work and O(n+s*m) storage without retaining the full matrix. Investigate fusing parsing, row-window generation, eviction and maximum updates. Use one portable bounded-buffer reader for all input, handling signed integers, legal whitespace and buffer/EOF boundaries; do not depend on seekable stdin or mix unsynchronized iostreams with stdio.",
    "G": "Integrate only components demonstrated by A/B/C. First identify the best complete standalone candidate, then measure compatible combinations such as C's reader with A's or B's kernel; do not assume gains add or blindly combine incompatible layouts. Evaluate every integrated binary through the unchanged correctness and timing process and reject regressions. Review negative-only matrices, s=1, s=n-1, ring initialization/eviction, wide intermediate differences and allocation sizes. Any dispatch must use a general cost model, never test identities, particular matrix values or expected answers. Preserve stdin/stdout, compiler, target, tests, oracle and all budgets.",
    "analysis": "The supplied source solves Problem B, maximum-sum fixed-size submatrix. Its serpentine traversal repeatedly scans s entries per move, including strided column accesses; separable rolling sums can reduce computation to O(n²). The baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.12606 seconds across three samples, but no profile or input-size distribution establishes the bottleneck. Explore serial streaming, four-core parallelism and input/memory efficiency independently on the ARM64 CPU target. All candidates must handle every valid input, initialize maxima correctly for negative-only matrices, and use wide intermediate arithmetic: a 32-bit final-answer guarantee does not bound partial sums."
